# Low-Level Design: LLM Chatbot System

---

## 1. Requirements

### 1.1 Functional Requirements

| # | Requirement | Description |
|---|---|---|
| FR1 | **Multi-Session Chats** | A user can create and manage multiple independent chat sessions |
| FR2 | **Multi-Turn Conversation** | The bot remembers previous messages within a session |
| FR3 | **Pluggable LLM Providers** | Support OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, or self-hosted models — switchable at runtime |
| FR4 | **Context Window Management** | Compress/prune history to fit within LLM token limits (e.g., 128K tokens) |
| FR5 | **Input & Output Guardrails** | Sanitize user prompts (PII masking, prompt injection blocking) and validate LLM responses (toxicity filtering) |
| FR6 | **Streaming Responses** | Stream tokens to the client in real-time using SSE (Server-Sent Events) |
| FR7 | **Fee/Token Tracking** | Track tokens consumed per session for billing purposes |

### 1.2 Non-Functional Requirements

| # | Requirement | Description |
|---|---|---|
| NFR1 | **Extensibility** | Adding a new LLM provider or guardrail should NOT require changing existing code |
| NFR2 | **Reliability** | Graceful fallback to a secondary LLM if the primary fails or rate-limits |
| NFR3 | **Concurrency** | Handle thousands of simultaneous sessions without data corruption |
| NFR4 | **Low Latency** | Use caching (Redis) to avoid slow DB reads on every message |

---

## 2. Out of Scope

Explicitly state these in the first 2 minutes of an interview to save time:

- **User Authentication/Authorization** — Assume a gateway passes a validated `userId`
- **LLM Training/Hosting** — We consume LLMs via API; we don't train or host models
- **Database Infra** — We design repository interfaces; sharding/replication details are out of scope
- **Frontend/UI** — We only design the backend API contract and class interactions
- **Vector DB Internals** — For RAG, we treat the Vector DB as a black-box service we call via an interface

