## 6. Storage Architecture

### Why 3 layers?

```
┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                   YOUR APP SERVER (Java/JVM)                      │
│                                                                   │
│   HashMap<sessionId, ChatSession>                                 │
│   ✅ Fastest (no network call)                                    │
│   ❌ Lost if this server crashes                                  │
│   ❌ Not shared across multiple server instances                  │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                            ↕ Network
┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                     REDIS (Separate Server)                       │
│                                                                   │
│   Stores data in RAM, but on a DIFFERENT machine                  │
│   ✅ Your app crashes → Redis still has the data                  │
│   ✅ All app server instances can read/write to same Redis        │
│   ⚠️  Redis crashes → data lost (unless AOF persistence enabled)  │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                            ↕ Network
┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                  POSTGRESQL (Separate Server)                     │
│                                                                   │
│   Stores data on DISK                                             │
│   ✅ Survives any crash (app, Redis, power outage)                │
│   ❌ Slowest (disk I/O + network)                                 │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### Read/Write Flow
```
WRITE: User sends message
  → Save to PostgreSQL (permanent record)
  → Also cache in Redis (for fast reads)

READ: Load context for LLM call
  → Try Redis first (fast, ~1ms)
  → If Redis miss → Load from PostgreSQL → Write back to Redis
```

> **One-liner**: Redis = speed layer, PostgreSQL = durability layer.

